 <u> SUBJECT </u> :    Education accountability:  high school exit  
examination

 <u> SOURCE </u> :     Author

                                                 CONTINUED





                                                      <u> SB 2X
 </u>                                                      Page  
2

 
 <u> DIGEST </u> :    This bill requires the State Superintendent of  
Public Instruction, with approval of the State Board of  
Education, to develop a high school exit examination in  
language arts and mathematics.  Requires high school  
pupils, beginning in 2004, to pass the exam in order to  
graduate.

 <u>Assembly Amendments </u> make numerous changes without altering  
the intent of the bill.  The changes include (a)  
appropriating $250,000 to the State Superintendent of  
Public Instruction to provide support services, (b) adding  
notification requirements, (c) providing for funding in the  
Budget Act for administration of the exam, (d) clarifying  
that English language learners are pupils who may receive  
supplemental instruction in order to pass the exam, and (e)  
making numerous technical and clarifying changes.

 <u> ANALYSIS </u> :    Currently, there are two statewide testing  
requirements concerning schools:

 <u> STAR (Standardized Testing and Reporting) (SB 376/1997) </u> .   
All students in grades 2 through 11 are tested in the  
spring of each year on a standardized,  
commercially-published ("off-the-shelf"), test of basic  
skills that is designated by the State Board of Education  
(SBE).  SBE has currently designated the Stanford  
Achievement Test, form 9 (SAT 9 or Stanford 9) test,  
published by Harcourt Brace.  This test provides individual  
student scores that are cross-referenced to the publisher's  
national test-taking population.

 <u> Local Proficiency Tests (AB 65/1977)</u> .  All school districts  
maintaining any of grades 6 through 12 are required to  
establish basic skills proficiency standards and administer  
proficiency assessments (usually tests) that all students  
must pass in order to graduate.  Tests and standards are  
locally developed and aligned to local curriculum, provided  
they address, at a minimum, "reading comprehension, writing  
and computational skills, in the English language".   
Different standards and testing procedures are authorized  
for special education pupils and other pupils with a  
diagnosed learning disability.  Assessment of pupil  
proficiency is required at least once each during grades 4  







                                                      <u> SB 2X
 </u>                                                      Page  
3

through 6, and 7 through 9, and twice during grades 10 and  
11.  Pupils of limited English proficiency (LEP) are not  
tested until they have received at least 24 months of  
instruction, including six months instruction in English.

 <u> Current Graduation Requirements </u> .  Current law requires  
California students to complete state and locally specified  
courses in order to graduate from high school.  Although  
attainment of local proficiency standards (described above)  
is required, there is currently no single statewide test  
required for high school graduation.

 <u> Academic Standards </u> .  Current law calls for the SBE to adopt  
content and performance standards, but does not mandate the  
adoption of those standards locally.  To date, SBE has  
adopted content standards for subjects and grade levels  
specified in statute.  None of the current tests are  
"aligned" to the state standards, although the STAR test  
will be augmented with the addition of standards based  
questions when it is administered in spring 1999.

 <u> Statewide Pupil Assessment Review (SPAR) </u> .  Current law  
calls for appointment of a six-member test review panel, a  
majority of whom are parents of K-12 public school  
children.  This panel is charged with reviewing statewide  
tests to ensure that test questions do not invite  
disclosure of personal beliefs or practices.

 <u> Property Rights and Test Design </u> .  In a lawsuit ( <u> Debra P. v.  
Turlington </u> ) that challenged the State of Florida over their  
exit exam, a federal Court of Appeals determined that  
students have a legal property interest in receiving a  
diploma (thus the state must have a legally defensible  
position if it denies a diploma to an otherwise qualified  
person).  The court laid out three principles that should  
be considered for high school exit exams:  (1) the test  
must not intentionally discriminate against a protected  
group or class, (2) a graduation test must be a fair  
measure of what students have been taught, that is, it must  
have "curricular validity", and (3) students must have  
adequate advance notice of test requirements.

This bill:








                                                      <u> SB 2X
 </u>                                                      Page  
4

  1. Requires the State Superintendent of Public  
    Instruction (SPI), with the approval of the SBE, to  
    develop a high school exit exam in language arts and  
    mathematics in accordance with the State Board adopted  
    academically rigorous content standards.

  2. Requires SBE to adopt a high school exit examination  
    that is aligned to state contents by October 1, 2000.

  3. Requires that all students in the graduating class of  
    2003-2004, and all later years, pass the exit  
    examination in order to receive a diploma of high  
    school graduation.

  4. Requires the SPI to appoint a High School Exit  
    Examination Standards panel to assist with the  
    development of the test.  The panel, among others, must  
    be comprised of teachers, administrators, school board  
    members, and the general public.  Members will serve  
    for two years without pay and shall be representative  
    of the state's ethnic, geographical, and cultural  
    diversity and must have gender balance.

  5. Requires that the exit examination be field tested  
    prior to implementation to ensure that the examination  
    is free from bias and that its content is valid and  
    reliable.

  6. Requires that the exit examination be reviewed by the  
    existing Statewide Pupil Assessment Review (SPAR) Panel  
    to ensure that no questions elicit information  
    concerning personal beliefs or practices.

  7. Requires that the high school exit exam (a) not be  
    administered to a pupil who did not receive adequate  
    notice (as defined) regarding the test; (b) not  
    intentionally discriminate against a protected group or  
    class; (c) have instructional or curricular validity;  
    and (d) be scored as a criterion referenced exam.  If  
    this is not done, the test shall not be required as a  
    condition of graduation.

  8. Provides for accommodation of special education  
    students by requiring the development of differential  







                                                      <u> SB 2X
 </u>                                                      Page  
5

    standards and assessment procedures.

  9. Requires that each pupil takes the exit exam in grade  
    ten starting in 2002 and be allowed to take the exam at  
    each subsequent administration until each part of the  
    exam has been passed.  Pupils are also allowed, but not  
    required, to take the test in grade nine starting in  
    2001.

 10. Allows districts to defer testing in English Language  
    Learners (ELL) until the pupil has been in California  
    schools for two years, during which time the ELL pupil  
    should have received at least six months of instruction  
    in reading, writing and comprehension in the English  
    language.  Deferring test administration does not  
    exempt the ELL pupil from taking the exit test, and the  
    pupil must still pass the test in English in order to  
    graduate.

 11. Requires that supplemental instruction be provided to  
    any pupil who "does not demonstrate sufficient progress  
    toward passing the exit examination."  Specifies that  
    sufficient progress shall be determined on the basis of  
    either the results of STAR and the minimum levels of  
    proficiency specified by the SBE or the pupil's grades  
    and other indicators of academic achievement designated  
    by the school district.

 12. Effective January 1, 2000 repeals existing  
    requirements for establishment of local proficiency  
    standards and assessment of pupils to those standards.

 13. Appropriates $2 million of federal Goals 2000 funding  
    to the SPI to develop the high school exit examination.  
     Appropriates $250,000 to the SPI to provide support  
    services.

 14. Requires that by January 15, 2000, the SPI shall  
    contract for a multiyear independent evaluation of the  
    high school exit examination.  The evaluation shall be  
    based upon information gathered in field testing and  
    annual administrations of the examination and shall  
    include specified information.








                                                      <u> SB 2X
 </u>                                                      Page  
6

 15. Provides that after adoption and initial  
    administration of the high school exit examination the  
    SBE, in consultation with the SPI, shall study the  
    appropriateness of other criteria by which high school  
    pupils who are regarded as highly proficient but unable  
    to pass the high school exit examination may  
    demonstrate their competency and receive a high school  
    diploma.  These criteria may include, but are not  
    limited to, an exemplary academic records as evidenced  
    by transcripts and alternative tests of equal rigor in  
    the academic areas covered by the high school exit  
    examination.  If the SBE determines that other criteria  
    are appropriate and do not undermine the intent of this  
    chapter that all high school graduates demonstrate  
    satisfactory academic proficiency, the board shall  
    forward its recommendations to the Legislature for  
    enactment.

 16. Authorizes schools to use regularly available  
    resources and any available supplemental remedial  
    resources to prepare pupils to succeed on the exit  
    exam.

 <u> Comments

 </u> This bill is one of four bills sponsored by the Governor as  
part of his call for an extraordinary session of the  
Legislature dedicated to education reform issues.  The  
other three bills address accountability (SB 1X - Alpert),  
peer assistance and review for teachers (AB 1X -  
Villaraigosa), and reading reform (AB 2X - Mazzoni).

 <u> FISCAL EFFECT </u> :    Appropriation:  Yes   Fiscal Com.:  Yes    
Local:  Yes



According to the Assembly Appropriations Committee:



1.The bill appropriates $2 million in federal funds (Goals  
  2000) for the development of the high school exit exam.   
  These funds are provided in the proposed 1999 Budget Act.







                                                      <u> SB 2X
 </u>                                                      Page  
7




2.General Fund (non-Proposition 98) costs of $500,000  
  annually to contract out for a multi-year evaluation.



3.General Fund (non-Proposition 98) cost of $300,000  
  annually to the SPI to administer the development of the  
  test and the contract for an independent evaluation.



4.General Fund (Proposition 98) costs of $3-5 million  
  annually to high school districts with high schools to  
  offer the test to high school students in the class of  
  2003-04, beginning in 2001-02.



5.General Fund (Proposition 98) costs of $350,000 annually  
  to school districts for a parental notification,  
  beginning in 2001-02.  The bill states 2003-04, but it  
  should state 2001-02.

